<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/operating-systems-basics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The operating system is the <strong>referee and landlord between programs and hardware</strong>: it
    decides who runs, who gets memory, who touches the disk, and what happens when things crash. It exists
    because programs cannot be trusted to share nicely — one pointer bug or greedy loop would otherwise
    take down the whole machine. This lesson covers the syllabus's software unit at depth: the software
    landscape and its translator layers, the interrupt mechanism that makes an OS possible, then the three
    great abstractions — processes, virtual memory, files — with the scheduling and concurrency machinery
    that runs them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four jobs of every operating system" widget="equationStep" config={osCfg} />

  <h2>Software types, translators, and the interrupt</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>System software</strong> (operating systems, drivers, utilities) manages the machine;
    <strong>application software</strong> does the user's work — and the boundary is the OS's API. Programs
    reach it only through <strong>system calls</strong> (open a file, spawn a process, allocate memory):
    a few hundred doorways through which everything "the computer does" funnels, each entailing a
    privilege switch from user mode to kernel mode. The unit's language topics slot in here: source code in
    high-level languages is translated by <strong>compilers</strong> (whole program → machine code ahead of
    time — fast execution, platform-specific), <strong>interpreters</strong> (line by line at run time —
    portable, slower), or bytecode + virtual machines (Java, Python's model — the compromise); assemblers
    translate the thin human-readable layer above raw machine code. An IDE bundles editor, compiler,
    debugger and autocompletion around this pipeline.
  </p>
  <p>
    The mechanism that makes an operating system possible is the <strong>interrupt</strong>. Hardware
    events — a key pressed, a disk finishing a read, a network packet arriving, a timer expiring — signal
    the CPU, which suspends the current program, saves its state, and runs a handler. The humble
    <strong>timer interrupt</strong> (typically 100–1,000 times per second) is the heartbeat of
    multitasking: it returns control to the OS no matter what a program is doing, which is the only reason
    one runaway program cannot own the machine forever. Software interrupts (traps) are how system calls
    are implemented; a page fault is an interrupt raised by the memory unit. Interrupts convert "the OS is
    in charge" from a slogan into an electrical fact.
  </p>

  <h2>Processes and scheduling, with numbers</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>process</strong> is a program in execution: private virtual address space, open files, and
    at least one thread of control (modern programs split work into <em>threads</em> sharing one address
    space). The scheduler cycles through ready processes so rapidly — context switches cost roughly 1–10
    microseconds, plus cache disturbance — that dozens of programs appear to run simultaneously; on a
    multicore chip, a few genuinely do. Policies differ in what they optimise, and the difference is
    enormous. Worked example — three jobs arrive in order A (burst 8 ms), B (4 ms), C (1 ms).
    <strong>FCFS</strong> runs A, B, C: waits 0, 8, 12 — average <strong>6.7 ms</strong>.
    <strong>SJF</strong> (shortest job first) runs C, B, A: waits 0, 1, 5 — average <strong>2 ms</strong>.
    Same total work, less than a third of the waiting: scheduling policy alone nearly triples apparent
    speed. But SJF needs to know the future (estimated from history) and starves long jobs, so real
    systems blend ideas: <strong>round robin</strong> gives each ready job a time quantum (fairness),
    <strong>priority queues</strong> separate interactive from batch work (responsiveness), and modern
    schedulers (Linux CFS) allocate virtual runtime with weighted fairness — interactive tasks feel
    instant while backups grind on unwatched.
  </p>

  <h2>Virtual memory: the illusion that pays for itself</h2>
  <p>
    Virtual memory gives every process the <strong>illusion of owning all of RAM</strong>, translated to
    physical frames page by page (typically 4 KB each) through page tables — with a <strong>TLB</strong>
    cache making the common translation nearly free. Three wins: <strong>protection</strong> (one process
    physically cannot read another's memory — the hardware enforces what goodwill could not),
    <strong>overcommitment</strong> (total virtual memory may exceed physical RAM; cold pages migrate to
    disk and return on demand via page faults — a fault costs milliseconds against ~100 nanoseconds for a
    RAM access, roughly a 10,000× penalty, which is why <strong>thrashing</strong>, the death spiral where
    the system spends its life swapping, is the classic performance emergency), and <strong>shared
    libraries</strong> mapped into many address spaces at once. Copy-on-write fork, memory-mapped files,
    and zero-filled pages are all refinements of the same illusion — memory stopped being an address and
    became a managed cache.
  </p>

  <h2>Concurrency: the price of parallelism</h2>
  <p>
    The moment two threads touch shared state, timing enters the semantics: a <strong>race condition</strong>
    is two interleaved updates whose result depends on the schedule — the classic lost update (both threads
    read the balance, both write, one increment vanishes). The fix is making critical sections
    <strong>indivisible</strong> — locks, atomic instructions — and the fix's own hazard is
    <strong>deadlock</strong>: threads holding one lock while waiting for another in a cycle (the dining
    philosophers is the canonical cartoon). Deadlock needs four conditions simultaneously (mutual
    exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait), which is why real systems prevent it by
    breaking one — lock ordering above all. The operating system itself lives with concurrency: every
    kernel data structure touched by an interrupt handler is a shared resource, which is why kernel
    engineering is, substantially, the art of lock discipline.
  </p>

  <h2>Files: one interface for a universe of devices</h2>
  <p>
    The file abstraction hides astonishing variety: SSDs, spinning disks, USB sticks and network shares
    all appear as trees of named bytes, with <strong>drivers</strong> translating the generic requests and
    the <strong>file system</strong> (ext4, NTFS, APFS) managing allocation, directories and metadata.
    The subtle part is crash consistency: writing a file touches many disk locations, and power can fail
    between them — so modern file systems <strong>journal</strong>, recording intended changes in a log
    before applying them, so a crash mid-write can be rolled back or replayed instead of corrupting the
    volume. The OS adds permissions (who may read/write/execute — the basis of every multi-user security
    model), caching (pages of files stay in RAM — "free memory is wasted memory"), and the uniform pipe
    that lets one program's output become another's input.
  </p>

  <h2>The boot sequence, and the memory hierarchy in numbers</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>How the referee gets onto the field.</strong> Power-on: the CPU starts executing firmware
    (UEFI, the successor to BIOS), which initialises hardware, verifies the boot chain (secure boot
    checks signatures), and loads the <strong>bootloader</strong> (GRUB and friends); the bootloader
    loads the <strong>kernel</strong> image into memory, hands over control, and the kernel mounts the
    root file system and starts <strong>init</strong> — the first process (systemd on most modern Linux),
    which spawns everything else: drivers, services, the login manager, your session. Every subsequent
    program is a child of that chain — a fact that makes the boot log a map of the whole architecture.
    <strong>The hierarchy the referee manages.</strong> The numbers explain most of computing's
    performance folklore: an L1 cache reference ≈ 1 nanosecond; main RAM ≈ 100 ns; an SSD read ≈
    100 microseconds (1,000× RAM); a spinning disk seek ≈ 10 ms (100,000× RAM); a network round trip
    to another continent ≈ 150 ms. Each level is smaller and faster than the one below, and the OS plus
    hardware keep the fastest levels filled with what is likely to be needed next. <strong>Locality is
    the programmer's only lever</strong>: touching nearby data together (array walks, not pointer
    chases; processing records in file order) is worth 10–100× at zero cost, which is why "naive but
    contiguous" routinely beats "clever but scattered" — the physics behind the data-structures
    lesson's cache warning, and the reason "free memory is wasted memory" (idle RAM is doing cache
    duty).
  </p>

  <h2>Process states, signals, and the lifetime of a program</h2>
  <p>
    A process is not just "running" — the scheduler's world is a small state machine. A process is
    <strong>running</strong> (on a core), <strong>ready</strong> (runnable, waiting for the scheduler's
    nod), or <strong>blocked</strong> (waiting for disk, network, or a lock — no CPU until the event
    arrives); a blocked process consumes no time slices, which is why a thousand idle connections cost
    almost nothing while a thousand busy loops cost everything. The kernel steers this machine with
    <strong>signals</strong> — software interrupts delivered to processes: SIGTERM asks politely (cleanup
    handlers run), SIGKILL cannot be caught (the kernel simply reclaims the address space), SIGSEGV is
    the segment fault itself, delivered when your process touches an unmapped page — the OS enforcing
    the virtual-memory illusion with prejudice. The lifetime ends with <strong>zombies</strong> (a
    process that exited but whose status no one has collected — a parent's bookkeeping failure) and
    <strong>orphans</strong> (re-parented to init when the parent dies first). Process creation is
    stranger than it looks in textbooks: on Unix, a process <em>clones itself</em> (fork — same memory,
    copied) and the clone replaces itself with a new program (exec) — the shell's whole job, which is
    why understanding fork/exec is understanding how the command line works.
  </p>

  <h2>Threads versus processes, decided</h2>
  <p>
    The concurrency units differ in exactly one resource and it determines everything. A
    <strong>process</strong> owns a private address space: isolation by construction (a crash or pointer
    bug cannot touch another process — the OS must intercede for any sharing, via pipes, sockets or
    shared-memory segments), but creation and context-switching are expensive (new page tables, cold
    caches). A <strong>thread</strong> lives inside a process sharing its address space: creation and
    switching are cheap (same page tables, warm caches) and communication is trivial (just write the
    variable) — with the price that everything in this lesson's concurrency section now applies: races,
    locks, deadlocks, and one stray pointer corrupting a <em>sibling</em> thread rather than failing
    safely. The design trade has a name in every architecture: web servers historically forked a process
    per connection (isolation, heavy) and evolved to thread pools and event loops (efficiency, shared
    state to guard); browsers sandbox each <em>site</em> in its own process (Chrome's model) precisely to
    buy the isolation back, running threads inside each sandbox for speed. When you choose between
    threads and processes, you are choosing where to spend on isolation versus where to spend on
    communication — the same dial, redrawn at every scale from microcontrollers to data centres.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Kernel architectures</strong> carry the design argument: monolithic kernels (Linux) run
    everything in kernel mode for speed at the price of a driver bug crashing the machine; microkernels
    (Minix, seL4, QNX) keep a minimal core and push drivers to user-space servers for isolation at a
    message-passing cost; hybrid designs (Windows, macOS's XNU) compromise. The verified microkernel
    seL4 — formally proven correct against its specification — is the field's proof that the dream is
    achievable. <strong>Real-time scheduling</strong> inverts the desktop's goals: for an airbag controller
    or engine ECU, a rare 20 ms miss is failure — real-time operating systems (RTOSes) run in kilobytes
    and guarantee deadlines, a different universe from throughput-optimised servers where Linux runs for
    years without rebooting. <strong>Virtualisation</strong> and containers are virtual memory's generalisation:
    hypervisors give each guest OS the same illusion of owning real hardware that the OS gives processes;
    containers share a kernel but isolate views. Open questions: how should schedulers handle heterogeneous
    big.LITTLE cores and GPUs (the scheduler is becoming a resource broker across accelerators); can
    kernel attack surfaces shrink as security pressure grows; and as data centres become "one computer,"
    does the OS boundary move up a level — the scheduling question of the next decade.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Tanenbaum, <em>Modern Operating Systems</em>; Arpaci-Dusseau, <em>Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces</em>; Silberschatz, <em>Operating System Concepts</em>.</p>

